>| the baddies on the level.  Have this work very infrequently for
>+---------------
>
>Again, likewise for chaotics and possibly even for neutrals.

I disagree.  The very essense of chaoticness is that you are out for
yourself, not to help others.  Lawful sentients will pitch in and help
each other; orcs do *not* help little old ladies across the street!
Chaotics may be tameable via, say, promising them gold, but this will
work the same way for anybody who has gold.  And they may just as soon
decide to kill you and take your gold.

>I do wish there were a way to reverse
>"blessed" vs. "cursed" for chaotics without causing headaches.

Again, I disagree.  The very nature of the chaotic and lawful
alignments are *different* -- not just mirror reflections of each
other.  Chaotic gods should promote dissention, death and distruction,
pain and misery -- whatever it takes to promote their cause.  That is
why they are the source of curses and the undead.  The trick of
serving a chaotic god will thus be to always make sure you are a
better victimizer than victim.  In contrast, lawful gods will be 
interested in taking care of their followers.

Note that I have always associated lawful==good and chaotic==evil, and
my assessment of what various gods "should" act like is based upon
this.  In general, I find this association to be true of the monsters
in the game.

